    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Texas does not have a roof matching law. No statute or regulation requires an insurer to replace undamaged shingles so the new ones match the old. If someone tells you a Texas matching law entitles you to a new roof, ask them for the citation.</p>
<p>That doesn't mean you're stuck with a patchwork roof. In Texas, whether matching is owed turns on two things: what your policy says, and the facts on your roof. When the shingles are discontinued, too brittle to repair, or damaged across most of the slopes, a full replacement is often the only repair that actually restores the roof. That is the argument that works, and it is built on evidence, not on a law that doesn't exist.</p>

<h2>Start with your policy wording</h2>
<p>Pull your full policy, not just the declarations page, and look for three things.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The loss settlement language.</strong> Many Texas homeowners policies promise to pay the cost to repair or replace with material of "like kind and quality." If a repair can only be done with shingles that don't look or perform like the originals, you have a reasonable argument that it doesn't meet that standard.</li>
<li><strong>Anything about appearance.</strong> Some policies refer to a "reasonable uniform appearance" or similar. Others say the opposite: that the insurer won't pay to replace undamaged property because of a mismatch in color, texture or style.</li>
<li><strong>Endorsements that change the roof.</strong> A cosmetic damage exclusion, an ACV roof schedule, or a matching limitation endorsement can override the general language. These are often added at renewal, so check the current policy, not the one you bought years ago.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><p><strong>Read the whole policy:</strong> two homeowners on the same street, with the same carrier, can have different matching outcomes because one renewed onto a form with a matching limitation and the other didn't. If you can't find your policy, ask the carrier for a certified copy in writing.</p></div>

<h2>Why roofs are hard to match</h2>
<p>Even a shingle with the same name and color from the same manufacturer usually won't match a roof that has weathered for ten years. Granules fade and wash off at different rates on south and west slopes than on north slopes. Color blends are reformulated. A repaired patch can stand out for years.</p>
<p>Appearance isn't the only issue. Shingles from different product lines may differ in size, thickness, and how they seal, which affects whether new shingles can be woven into the old ones without leaks.</p>

<h2>Discontinued shingles</h2>
<p>When the original shingle is no longer made, the matching question becomes a repairability question, and that is often the stronger argument. To make it:</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Identify the shingle.</strong> Check the attic, garage or shed for a leftover bundle with its wrapper. If there isn't one, a roofer can pull a sample and compare it to manufacturer catalogs, or have it identified by a shingle identification service.</li>
<li><strong>Measure the exposure.</strong> Older "English" size shingles show less of each course than the "metric" size sold today. When the exposure doesn't line up, the new shingles can't course correctly into the old ones.</li>
<li><strong>Get availability in writing.</strong> A supplier letter or manufacturer confirmation that the product is discontinued carries far more weight than a roofer's statement.</li>
<li><strong>Show the closest substitute.</strong> Photograph the nearest available shingle against the existing roof in daylight. Let the carrier see the difference rather than argue about it.</li>
</ol>
<p>Some carriers respond by offering to search for a close match, or by paying for a replacement slope only. Neither ends the discussion if the closest available product is a different size or style.</p>

<h2>Repairability and brittleness</h2>
<p>A repair means lifting the shingles around the damaged ones, pulling nails, sliding in new shingles and resealing. If the surrounding shingles break when lifted, the repair destroys more roof than it fixes. That is the logic behind a brittleness test.</p>
<p>Testing methods vary, but the idea is the same: a roofer or adjuster tries to unseal and lift several tabs, the way a repair crew would, and watches what happens. If tabs crack, tear at the nail line, or the seal pulls up the mat underneath, the shingles can't be repaired without collateral damage. To make the result count:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>Test on more than one slope, and away from the damaged areas.</li>
<li>Record the test on video, with the time and outdoor temperature noted. Asphalt shingles are stiffer in cool weather, and carriers will raise it.</li>
<li>Photograph each failed tab next to an intact one.</li>
<li>Invite the carrier's adjuster to watch or repeat the test.</li>
</ul>
<p>Brittleness is a fact about your roof, so it is far harder for a carrier to dismiss than a general complaint about appearance.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Slopes versus the whole roof</h2>
<p>Carriers commonly approve damage slope by slope: the west and north slopes faced the storm and are replaced, the east and south are left alone. Whether that's fair depends on the roof.</p>
<div class="compare">
<div><h3>Arguments for slope-by-slope</h3><ul><li>Damage really is limited to the storm-facing slopes</li><li>The undamaged slopes are on another plane and can't be seen with the new ones</li><li>Matching shingles are available and the old ones are repairable</li></ul></div>
<div><h3>Arguments for the whole roof</h3><ul><li>Hail damage is present on every slope, even if less dense on some</li><li>Slopes meet at hips and ridges where a mismatch is plainly visible</li><li>The shingles are discontinued or fail a brittleness test</li><li>Ridge cap and hip shingles run continuously over approved and unapproved slopes</li></ul></div>
</div>
<p>The strongest evidence here is a slope-by-slope damage record: photos of chalked hits on each slope, with notes on what was found. Carriers often decide each slope using their own internal hit-count thresholds; our article on <a href="/post/how-insurance-companies-evaluate-hail-damage/">how insurance companies evaluate hail damage</a> explains those test squares. A slope the carrier skipped is often a slope nobody tested carefully.</p>

<h2>When a full replacement is justified</h2>
<p>Pulling the threads together, a full replacement is usually the right outcome when one or more of these is true and documented:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>Covered hail or wind damage on most or all slopes.</li>
<li>The existing shingle is discontinued and no available product is the same size and style.</li>
<li>The surrounding shingles fail a brittleness or repairability test.</li>
<li>The policy's loss settlement language supports like kind and quality or uniform appearance, and no endorsement limits it.</li>
<li>The cost of repairing damaged slopes approaches the cost of replacing the roof.</li>
</ul>
<p>Two situations work against you even with real damage. A metal roof with a cosmetic damage exclusion may not be covered for dents at all; see <a href="/post/cosmetic-vs-functional-hail-damage/">cosmetic versus functional hail damage</a>. And on a policy with an ACV roof endorsement, a full replacement may be approved but paid at a depreciated value.</p>

<h2>Matching beyond the roof</h2>
<p>The same principles apply to siding, gutters, fences and interior finishes. Vinyl and fiber cement siding are frequently discontinued, and a few replaced panels on one wall can be obvious. Flooring that runs continuously through several rooms raises the same question after a water loss. In each case, the policy language and the facts decide it, not a statute.</p>

<h2>How to ask for a full replacement</h2>
<p>Put the request in writing to your adjuster and make it specific: the policy language you rely on, the shingle identification and availability letter, the brittleness test video, and slope-by-slope damage photos. Ask for a written response on each point. If the carrier still approves only part of the roof and the disagreement is over the scope and amount of the loss, <a href="/post/appraisal-for-hail-damage-claim/">appraisal</a> may be the next step. We build these requests as part of our <a href="/services/roof-hail-damage-claims/">roof and hail claim work</a>, starting with a free review of the policy and the roof.</p>
